WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.075
This is Allen Company first-aid training on heat

00:07.075 --> 00:09.838
illness, followed by scenario drills.

00:09.838 --> 00:12.675
Kentucky summers on a bench, in a plant or in a

00:12.675 --> 00:15.750
shop can push your body past what it can handle.

00:16.896 --> 00:18.834
This is first-aid awareness training.

00:18.834 --> 00:22.322
It follows first-aid methods acceptable to MSHA,

00:22.322 --> 00:24.871
based on the American Heart Association first-aid

00:24.871 --> 00:27.734
guidelines that Allen uses for hands-on training.

00:27.734 --> 00:30.697
It supports, but does not replace, your hands-on

00:30.697 --> 00:33.059
first-aid practice with the safety manager,

00:33.059 --> 00:35.034
the same day as this course.

00:36.188 --> 00:39.975
Heat exhaustion: heavy sweating, cool clammy skin,

00:39.975 --> 00:43.125
dizziness, headache, nausea, cramps,

00:43.125 --> 00:46.888
weakness. Heat stroke is a life-threatening emergency:

00:46.888 --> 00:49.838
the person becomes confused, slurs speech,

00:49.838 --> 00:52.763
passes out or has a seizure, and their skin is

00:52.763 --> 00:55.938
very hot. Any change in how someone thinks or acts

00:55.938 --> 00:59.275
in the heat means heat stroke until proven otherwise.

01:00.483 --> 01:04.283
For heat exhaustion: move to shade or air conditioning,

01:04.283 --> 01:06.945
loosen clothing, give sips of cool water if they're

01:06.945 --> 01:10.545
alert, and rest. If they get worse or don't improve,

01:10.545 --> 01:14.358
get medical help. For heat stroke: call 911,

01:14.358 --> 01:16.820
then start cooling right away. The best way is

01:16.820 --> 01:19.208
to put them in cold water up to the chin.

01:19.208 --> 01:21.858
If you can't, use cold water, ice packs at the

01:21.858 --> 01:25.370
neck, armpits and groin, wet cloths and fanning.

01:25.370 --> 01:27.745
Don't give fluids if they're not fully alert.

01:28.878 --> 01:32.328
Prevent it: drink water often, before you're thirsty.

01:32.328 --> 01:35.066
Take breaks in shade. New workers and people back

01:35.066 --> 01:37.541
from time off need a few days to get used to the

01:37.541 --> 01:40.316
heat. Watch your coworkers, because people with

01:40.316 --> 01:43.253
heat stroke often don't know it. At Allen this

01:43.253 --> 01:46.216
is everyday practice, not a written plan,

01:46.216 --> 01:47.841
so it's up to each of us.

01:51.172 --> 01:53.522
Every one of these starts the same way:

01:53.522 --> 01:55.772
make the scene safe, call for help,

01:55.772 --> 01:57.909
and then do what you've practiced. Know where your

01:57.909 --> 02:00.934
first-aid kit, eyewash, stretcher and blankets

02:00.934 --> 02:02.309
are before you need them.

02:03.430 --> 02:06.592
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

02:06.592 --> 02:08.092
override this video.
